From: Escapement
Subject: [PATCH] linux_boot: give the kernel a command line this board can use

Two problems, one command line.

1. "quiet". The dts bootargs carry it, which drops the console log level to 4
   and suppresses almost every kernel message. This watch has exactly one
   output path -- ttyGS3, the S3's own USB-Serial-JTAG -- so "quiet" turns a
   booting kernel into silence, and silence is indistinguishable from a kernel
   that never started. Two sessions were spent reading core 1's program counter
   over JTAG to guess whether Linux was alive, when the kernel was talking the
   whole time.

2. Console order. The dts says "console=ttyGS3 console=ttyS0,115200n8", and
   /dev/console binds to the LAST console= on the line -- ttyS0, a UART this
   board does not have. printk mirrors to every registered console, so the log
   appeared on USB while init's stdin/stdout went to the dead UART. A shell was
   running and reading from hardware that was not connected; typing into the
   watch produced nothing because nothing was listening on the wire that had
   the finger on it.

   Putting ttyGS3 last fixes it. This is also what makes the stock /etc/inittab
   work unmodified: it contains "console::respawn:/sbin/getty -L console 0
   vt100", and with ttyGS3 last, /dev/console IS the USB port, so a login
   appears with no editing of the etc partition. Upstream's documented
   "usb-console on" step exists because on their devkit ttyS0 is last and
   /dev/console is the UART; here there is no UART to fall back to.

The firmware supplies this only when the etc partition has no /etc/cmdline, so
a user-provided command line still wins.

Verified on hardware 2026-11-08: "buildroot login:" appears on /dev/ttyACM1 at
115200, and logging in as root reaches the password prompt.

diff --git a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c
--- a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c
+++ b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c
@@ -33,6 +33,29 @@ static const esp_partition_t *find_partition(const char *name)
 #define CMDLINE_MAX 260
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_LINUX_COMMAND_LINE
+/* Escapement: a command line for a board with no reachable console.
+ *
+ * The built-in bootargs in the dts carry "quiet", which drops the console log
+ * level to 4 and suppresses nearly every kernel message. On a board whose only
+ * output path is ttyGS3 -- the S3's own USB-Serial-JTAG -- that turns a booting
+ * kernel into silence, and silence is indistinguishable from a kernel that
+ * never started.
+ *
+ * This mirrors the dts bootargs exactly, minus "quiet", and adds loglevel=7 at
+ * the end so it takes effect whether the kernel treats this tag as a
+ * replacement for the built-in command line or as an append to it. A real
+ * /etc/cmdline still wins if one exists on the etc partition.
+ */
+static const char default_cmdline[] =
+	"earlycon=esp32s3uart,mmio32,0x60000000,115200n8,40000000 "
+	/* ttyGS3 last on purpose: /dev/console binds to the LAST console= on the
+	 * line. With ttyS0 last, init's stdin/stdout went to a UART this board
+	 * does not have, while printk still mirrored to ttyGS3 -- so the log
+	 * appeared and typing went nowhere. */
+	"console=ttyS0,115200n8 console=ttyGS3 "
+	"rw root=mtd:rootfs rootfstype=cramfs panic=10 panic_console_replay "
+	"loglevel=7";
+
 static void parse_cmdline(const void *ptr, uint32_t size, struct bp_tag tag[])
 {
 	struct jffs2_image img = {
@@ -40,17 +63,26 @@ static void parse_cmdline(const void *ptr, uint32_t size, struct bp_tag tag[])
 		.sz = size,
 	};
 	uint32_t cmdline_inode = jffs2_lookup(&img, 1, "cmdline");
+	char *cmdline = (char *)tag[1].data;
 
 	if (cmdline_inode) {
-		char *cmdline = (char *)tag[1].data;
 		size_t rd = jffs2_read(&img, cmdline_inode, cmdline, CMDLINE_MAX - 1);
 
-		if (rd != -1) {
+		if (rd != (size_t)-1) {
 			tag[1].id = BP_TAG_COMMAND_LINE;
 			cmdline[rd] = 0;
 			ESP_LOGI(__func__, "found /etc/cmdline [%d] = '%s'\n", rd, cmdline);
+			return;
 		}
 	}
+
+	size_t n = strlen(default_cmdline);
+	if (n > CMDLINE_MAX - 1)
+		n = CMDLINE_MAX - 1;
+	memcpy(cmdline, default_cmdline, n);
+	cmdline[n] = 0;
+	tag[1].id = BP_TAG_COMMAND_LINE;
+	ESP_LOGI(__func__, "no /etc/cmdline, default = '%s'\n", cmdline);
 }
 #endif
 
